# Allocation of Funds for Industrial Development
Tamil title: தொழில் வளர்ச்சிக்கான நிதி ஒதுக்கீடு

Speech delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 24 July 1957 during the grants debate.

Mr Speaker, while supporting the cut motion moved upon the grant allotted to the Industries Department, I wish to offer a few observations to spur the Government to devote still greater attention to industry. I ask members of the ruling party to receive my remarks in that spirit.

Generally speaking, this Government has no sound industrial plan. Yet, even while making that charge, I can argue on its behalf: it has no plan because responsibility for framing one does not rest with it. If those holding responsibility for the State administration would recognise that the power to devise plans lies elsewhere, and temper their words and actions accordingly, this State would have better prospects of forming good industrial plans.

I do not cite national separation as my basis, since doing so may cause the rulers some discomfort. Even without pressing that argument, I would draw attention to what is generally done in Japan. Regional boards there devise industrial plans suited to each region's capacity, climate and people's income, while ensuring that the plans do not conflict and that common arrangements remain possible. I bring that practice to the Government's attention.

That we are far behind in industry is not a partisan allegation for one party to make and another to deny. Members of every party know that the South is industrially backward. The whole country knows that the South was neglected in the First Five-Year Plan and that, even in the Second Plan, our State Government returned dismayed, unable to obtain the sum it had requested because southern development was not treated as important.

When economic planning is conducted solely on an all-India basis, some areas are turned into famine regions, not necessarily by intention but through the impossibility of meeting every region's needs with one plan. To use a homely example, when bathing the whole body, one still needs another's help to scrub the back. In the same way, a countrywide plan leaves particular regions unattended.

Government statistics themselves say that in Bombay State there are 442 earners and 57 dependants, while in Madras State there are only 41 earners for 69 people who must be supported. Thus, fewer people earn here and more must live upon what they earn. The same figures state that a southern cultivator works 187 days a year while his northern counterpart works 292 days, and that annual agricultural income is Rs 382 in the South and Rs 512 in the North. Whatever the qualifications surrounding such statistics, one truth emerges from the Government's own figures: the South remains disadvantaged.

When we state that truth, their hearts begin to pound. They evade the issue by saying, "We are all one; all India is one. We conduct affairs on that basis, whereas you approach questions with a divisive and discriminatory mind." No lofty argument, whoever utters it, can be accepted if it leaves the South depressed. The South cannot progress unless its deficiencies are remedied, and no political sophistry can alter that.

I recall what the economist P. J. Thomas, formerly of Madras, said last week. In both the First and Second Five-Year Plans, he observed, the North generally prospered more than the South. Southerners point to their lack of progress not from jealousy. He explained why prices had risen in one region, argued that the South required a plan to change the position, and said people free of political agitation should work upon it with a proper protective concern. What we have said about neglect of the South is now being voiced and supported on every side. It shows which way the wind is blowing and must not be dismissed as a joke.

The ruling party answers that it alone served the country and therefore alone possesses the right to decide what the country needs. We bow to those who laboured for the nation. Yet we must also examine how the administration conducted by those now in office actually functions, feel concern for it, and recover the country for a better course. Hope born of that concern does not belong only to their side; it can arise among those on this side too.

Through you, Mr Speaker, I make a request. We are asked in this Assembly, "We alone sacrificed for this country; what have you done?" As far as my party is concerned, if you wish to count those who were once with us, you may accept what is said, for a few of them are today in your ministry. As the saying goes, "The weary man's child becomes the mendicant of the Pillaiyar temple." Do not imagine that you may say anything whatever when we raise these questions. Please turn instead to solving them.

Before offering proposals, I wish to place before the House the industries that existed in 1945-57 according to the source cited in the printed record. The OCR rendering of the following English list is substantially illegible. A major part of those industries was lost to us through linguistic reorganisation of the provinces.

There is an oddity here. When those undertakings existed, many officials administered them; I now understand that this same department employs still more officials. If I am mistaken, the Minister must correct me. Where formerly there were a director and a joint director for the undivided territory, there are now reportedly a director, an additional director, three joint directors, numerous gazetted officers beneath them, and twenty or thirty engineers. The Minister should explain why. If the information is incorrect, it is equally his duty to set the record straight.

If more small industries are to be started, the Government must operate them directly. Private persons come forward when immediate and substantial profit is available, not from charitable concern that a thousand people should find employment or that the country and its economy should advance. I therefore cite a proposal that the Government immediately undertake a series of industries.

First is paper production. We are told that about eight hundred newspapers are now published in our country, yet paper has to be obtained from elsewhere. Since reed and bamboo forests needed for it are readily available here, production can begin at once.

Several further proposals appear as an English numbered list in the scan, but most entries have been destroyed by Tamil OCR. One clearly concerns silk. Members of the House have spoken movingly about it. At least to satisfy the women who wear it, the industry should be established without delay; it would sometimes save men the trouble involved in purchasing silk as well. This land sent silk to western countries for thousands of years. We should immediately try to expand its production.

Another recoverable proposal concerns using the fat and other substances that are now wasted in our many tanneries to manufacture useful products, as specialists have recommended. The speech continues with proposals involving metals and engineers, but entries four through thirteen of the printed English list are not reliably recoverable from this OCR text.

Mr Speaker, I turn aside from that list because I am obliged to answer certain remarks made in this House. While presenting the budget, our Finance Minister looked at me with his characteristic smile and said that four lakh rupees had been allotted to grow cashew plantations. He suggested this might satisfy those who speak in alliterative phrases about "sindhiri" and "mundhiri."

The Hon. C. Subramaniam: I said it might satisfy them to some extent.

C. N. Annadurai: When we say, "industry there, industry here; sindhiri there, mundhiri here," we do not merely pile up rhyming words. We speak so only after acquiring some skill in that style, and we also see whether the proposals can yield practical benefit. By the cashew industry we do not mean simply the cashew fruit. We mean developing an industry that uses by-products of the cashew plant to make articles such as plastics. That must be remembered.

Cashew plantations do not exist only in the Finance Minister's district. As a boy playing around Athur, I could obtain cashew fruit and eat as much as I wished. We are not asking the Minister merely to grow fruit. When we ask that the associated industry be developed, the Government may answer that it has no money.

If I then say, "Impose a tax when funds are lacking," the Congress whip may crack his whip at me and say, "When we levy taxes, do you not oppose them?" If a country is to be governed by taxation alone, there is no difference between a government and a charitable rest house or wayside shelter. Government revenue should have two sources: taxation, and public ownership of every field capable of producing income. Affection for an undertaking merely because it is in private or wealthy hands must not obstruct that policy.

Industry is now divided into the public and private sectors. In the private sector individual capitalists invest and operate enterprises; in the public sector the Government invests and runs them. Yet the Government sometimes gives loans and assistance to private enterprises, often according to the closeness between those in authority and those conducting the business.

That arrangement should change. Industries in our country should be placed in the public sector under two forms: some wholly financed and operated by the Government, and others established with the Government taking a controlling share and the remaining shares offered to the public. If people know that the Government itself will conduct an undertaking, they will buy shares with greater confidence than they place in private capitalists.

The small-savings campaign is promoted through songs, dances, plays, friends and, finally, officials. I suggest linking it to a visible productive purpose. Do not merely advertise, "Save your money; in twelve years Rs 700 will become Rs 765," like placing a mango sapling in a basket, removing the basket and asking people to admire what remains. Use those savings in a way that benefits them.

Earmark the money for enterprises such as the Salem aluminium factory and convert it into shares that help those industries prosper. The scheme would then succeed quickly. I ask the Government to carry it out in that manner.

After saying all this, I cannot conclude without expressing my dissatisfaction. It is directed neither at a person nor a party, but at the times themselves. Tamil Nadu, which once sent silk and pearls throughout the world, now sends labourers abroad. I lament the age that has brought this change.

Though we have been given ministers of energy, knowledge and ability, they cannot act because Delhi has not granted them adequate authority.

The Hon. C. Subramaniam: We do not send labourers abroad today. It may have happened in earlier times.

C. N. Annadurai: Foreign countries do not now accept labourers. Those who went from here to Ceylon are being driven out. Keeping in mind our economic condition and the views expressed by scholars who have examined it, let us use the powers available to us and make sound industrial progress. Poverty in our country is beyond description. Because it has reached that degree, I ask for genuine advancement in industry. Supporting this cut motion, I conclude my speech.

---NOTES---
The printed introduction and speech give the date as 24 July 1957. The generated OCR heading has been replaced with the printed Tamil title, while the complete OCR source remains unchanged in the bilingual output.

The OCR severely corrupts two English numbered lists: an inventory of earlier industries and thirteen proposed industries. Only paper, silk, tannery by-products, and a later reference to metals are recoverable with confidence from the surrounding Tamil explanation. The translation identifies the gaps rather than silently reconstructing them.

The OCR figures on earners, dependants, working days, and income contain possible digit errors. They are translated as displayed in the preserved source and should be checked against the scan before citation.

“சிந்திரி முந்திரி” is an alliterative political jibe involving the word for cashew; it is transliterated where Anna discusses the wordplay.
